IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cca-claims-dates`

-- Competition and Consumer Act 2010 (Cth) -- baseline encoding from the seat of the claimant: the individual hurt by
-- goods with a safety defect (or the relative, dependant or owner who suffers loss through it), and the person who
-- suffers loss because someone contravened Chapter 2 or 3 of the Australian Consumer Law (Schedule 2) and sues for it.
--
-- SEAT. The claimant ("I"). Questions are phrased as that person asks them. The manufacturer, the supplier, the
-- regulator, the Commonwealth and the court get no seat: what they must do is encoded as what it means for me, and the
-- court's judgments (safety defect, reasonable care, share of responsibility, the severity of non-economic loss, the
-- date of discoverability, an extension of the long-stop period) are inputs.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. The one duty with a deadline (ACL s 147: a supplier asked to name the manufacturer has 30 days) is a regulative
-- rule run inside a question.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: Schedule 2 (Australian Consumer Law) ss 7, 9 (as an input), 138-144, 146-150, 236, 237(1)-(3);
-- the Act's ss 137A, 137B, 137C, 137E; Part VIB ss 87D, 87E, 87F-87K, 87L-87S, 87U, 87W, 87X, 87Y, 87Z, 87ZA, 87ZB,
-- 87ZC. What is out, and why, is in NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 166, compilation date 27 August 2026 (register id C2026C00389), deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A00109.txt, with the s 87M and s 87Z formulas from C2004A00109.formulas.md. The
-- Competition and Consumer Regulations 2010 (deposited beside this Act) prescribe no discount rate (s 87Y), no interest
-- rate (s 87ZA) and no average weekly earnings substitute (s 87V(1)(b)), so the Act's own defaults apply. The CPI and
-- average weekly earnings figures the Australian Statistician publishes are not deposited, so each is an input.
--
-- Sourced from the Federal Register of Legislation, Commonwealth of Australia, CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05
-- (NOTES.md): JUST FALSE is the text's own no; NOTHING is where the text is silent or limited by its own wording.

§ `Competition and Consumer Act 2010 -- the claimant`

§§ `Who is the manufacturer (ACL s 7)`

-- s 7(1): "A manufacturer includes the following: (a) a person who grows, extracts, produces, processes or assembles
-- goods; (b) a person who holds himself or herself out to the public as the manufacturer of goods; (c) a person who causes
-- or permits the name of the person, a name by which the person carries on business or a brand or mark of the person to
-- be applied to goods supplied by the person; (d) a person ... who causes or permits another person, in connection with
-- [the other's supply or promotion of the goods], to hold out the first person to the public as the manufacturer ...;
-- (e) a person who imports goods into Australia if: (i) the person is not the manufacturer of the goods; and (ii) at the
-- time of the importation, the manufacturer of the goods does not have a place of business in Australia."
-- s 7(2)(b): if the name, brand or mark is applied, "it is presumed, unless the contrary is established, that the person
-- caused or permitted" it. s 7(3): goods imported on a person's behalf are imported by that person.
-- "includes": JUST TRUE where a head holds; NOTHING otherwise (an open list).
@export Is the business I want to sue a manufacturer of the goods (Australian Consumer Law s 7)? A manufacturer includes whoever makes, grows, processes or assembles the goods; holds itself out as the manufacturer; has its name or brand on goods it supplies (presumed to have permitted it unless it proves otherwise); lets another hold it out as the manufacturer; or imports the goods (or has them imported for it) when the maker has no place of business in Australia.
GIVEN `it makes the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business grows, extracts, produces, processes or assembles the goods
      `it holds itself out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business holds itself out to the public as the manufacturer of the goods
      `its name or brand is on goods it supplies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business's name, trading name, brand or mark is on the goods it supplies (woven in, stamped, attached, or on the packaging or label)
      `it proved it did not cause or permit that` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business has shown that it did not cause or permit its name or brand to be put on the goods
      `it lets another hold it out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business causes or permits another business that supplies or promotes the goods to present it to the public as the manufacturer
      `it imported the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business imported the goods into Australia, or had them imported on its behalf
      `it is not the maker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the importing business is not itself the maker of the goods
      `the maker had no Australian place of business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when the goods were imported, their maker had no place of business in Australia
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the business a manufacturer of the goods question` `it makes the goods` `it holds itself out` `its name or brand is on goods it supplies` `it proved it did not cause or permit that` `it lets another hold it out` `it imported the goods` `it is not the maker` `the maker had no Australian place of business` MEANS
    IF     `it makes the goods`
        OR `it holds itself out`
        OR (`its name or brand is on goods it supplies` AND (NOT `it proved it did not cause or permit that`))
        OR `it lets another hold it out`
        OR (`it imported the goods` AND `it is not the maker` AND `the maker had no Australian place of business`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Liability of manufacturers for goods with safety defects (ACL Part 3-5 Division 1)`

-- s 142: "In a defective goods action, it is a defence if it is established that: (a) the safety defect ... did not
-- exist: (i) in the case of electricity--at the time at which the electricity was generated ...; or (ii) in any other
-- case--at the time when the goods were supplied by their actual manufacturer; or (b) the goods had that safety defect
-- only because there was compliance with a mandatory standard for them; or (c) the state of scientific or technical
-- knowledge at the time when the goods were supplied by their manufacturer was not such as to enable that safety defect
-- to be discovered; or (d) if the goods ... were comprised in other goods--that safety defect is attributable only to:
-- (i) the design of the other goods; or (ii) the markings ...; or (iii) the instructions or warnings given by the
-- manufacturer of the other goods."
GIVEN `defect not there at supply` IS A BOOLEAN, `only because of a mandatory standard` IS A BOOLEAN
      `undiscoverable then` IS A BOOLEAN, `only the other goods' design or instructions` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 142 -- a defence is established` `defect not there at supply` `only because of a mandatory standard` `undiscoverable then` `only the other goods' design or instructions` IF
       `defect not there at supply`
    OR `only because of a mandatory standard`
    OR `undiscoverable then`
    OR `only the other goods' design or instructions`

DECLARE `Defective goods answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the manufacturer is liable to compensate me, and I may recover the amount of my loss or damage by action against it`
    `no: a condition of the section is not met`
    `no: the loss is one recovered, or recoverable, under a workers' compensation law or a law giving effect to an international agreement (s 146)`
    `no: the manufacturer has established a defence (s 142)`

-- The shared tail of ss 138-141: s 146 ("Division 1 does not apply to a loss or damage in respect of which an amount has
-- been, or could be, recovered under a law ... that: (a) relates to workers' compensation; or (b) gives effect to an
-- international agreement"), then the s 142 defences.
GIVEN `the conditions are met` IS A BOOLEAN, `workers' compensation covers it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `defect not there at supply` IS A BOOLEAN, `only because of a mandatory standard` IS A BOOLEAN
      `undiscoverable then` IS A BOOLEAN, `only the other goods' design or instructions` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Defective goods answer`
`the defective goods outcome` `the conditions are met` `workers' compensation covers it` `defect not there at supply` `only because of a mandatory standard` `undiscoverable then` `only the other goods' design or instructions` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the conditions are met`)
    THEN `no: a condition of the section is not met`
    ELSE IF `workers' compensation covers it`
         THEN `no: the loss is one recovered, or recoverable, under a workers' compensation law or a law giving effect to an international agreement (s 146)`
         ELSE IF `s 142 -- a defence is established` `defect not there at supply` `only because of a mandatory standard` `undiscoverable then` `only the other goods' design or instructions`
              THEN `no: the manufacturer has established a defence (s 142)`
              ELSE `yes: the manufacturer is liable to compensate me, and I may recover the amount of my loss or damage by action against it`

-- s 138(1): "A manufacturer of goods is liable to compensate an individual if: (a) the manufacturer supplies the goods in
-- trade or commerce; and (b) the goods have a safety defect; and (c) the individual suffers injuries because of the
-- safety defect." (2): "The individual may recover, by action against the manufacturer, the amount of the loss or damage
-- suffered by the individual." s 9(1): goods have a safety defect "if their safety is not such as persons generally are
-- entitled to expect" -- a judgment, asked as a fact.
@export I was injured by goods with a safety defect: is the manufacturer liable to compensate me (Australian Consumer Law ss 138, 142, 146)? The manufacturer is liable if it supplied the goods in trade or commerce, the goods have a safety defect (their safety is not what people generally are entitled to expect) and you were injured because of it, unless workers' compensation (or a law giving effect to an international agreement) covers the loss, or the manufacturer establishes a defence. If you die of the injuries, State or Territory law on death claims applies (s 138(3)). The court may reduce the amount for your own share in causing the loss (s 137A), and Part VIB of the Act limits personal injury damages.
GIVEN `it supplied the goods in trade or commerce` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manufacturer supplied the goods in trade or commerce (in the course of business)
      `the goods have a safety defect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the goods' safety is not such as people generally are entitled to expect (a court's judgment, having regard to the marketing, packaging, marks, instructions and warnings, expected use and the time of supply)
      `I was injured because of the defect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you suffered injuries because of the safety defect
      `workers' compensation covers it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an amount has been, or could be, recovered for this loss under a workers' compensation law, or under a law giving effect to an international agreement
      `defect not there at supply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manufacturer has established that the defect did not exist when the goods were supplied by their actual manufacturer (for electricity, when it was generated)
      `only because of a mandatory standard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manufacturer has established that the goods had the defect only because they complied with a mandatory standard
      `undiscoverable then` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manufacturer has established that scientific or technical knowledge when it supplied the goods could not have discovered the defect
      `only the other goods' design or instructions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the goods were a component of other goods and the manufacturer has established that the defect is due only to the other goods' design, markings, or the other manufacturer's instructions or warnings
GIVETH A `Defective goods answer`
`Is the manufacturer liable for my injuries question` `it supplied the goods in trade or commerce` `the goods have a safety defect` `I was injured because of the defect` `workers' compensation covers it` `defect not there at supply` `only because of a mandatory standard` `undiscoverable then` `only the other goods' design or instructions` MEANS
    `the defective goods outcome`
        (`it supplied the goods in trade or commerce` AND `the goods have a safety defect` AND `I was injured because of the defect`)
        `workers' compensation covers it` `defect not there at supply` `only because of a mandatory standard` `undiscoverable then` `only the other goods' design or instructions`

-- s 139(1): liable to compensate a person if "(a) the manufacturer supplies the goods in trade or commerce; and (b) the
-- goods have a safety defect; and (c) an individual (other than the person) suffers injuries because of the safety
-- defect; and (d) the person suffers loss or damage because of: (i) the injuries; or (ii) if the individual dies because
-- of the injuries--the individual's death; and (e) the loss or damage does not come about because of a business or
-- professional relationship between the person and the individual."
@export Someone else was injured or killed by goods with a safety defect and I suffered loss because of it: is the manufacturer liable to compensate me (Australian Consumer Law ss 139, 142, 146)? It is if it supplied the goods in trade or commerce, the goods have a safety defect, the other person was injured because of the defect, and you suffered loss because of their injuries or death, unless your loss comes from a business or professional relationship with them, workers' compensation covers it, or the manufacturer establishes a defence. Any reduction for the injured person's own share in causing the loss applies to your claim too (s 137A(1)).
GIVEN `it supplied the goods in trade or commerce` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manufacturer supplied the goods in trade or commerce (in the course of business)
      `the goods have a safety defect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the goods' safety is not such as people generally are entitled to expect (a court's judgment)
      `another person was injured because of the defect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another individual (not you) suffered injuries because of the safety defect
      `I suffered loss from their injuries or death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you suffered loss or damage because of that person's injuries or, if they died of them, their death
      `my loss comes from a business relationship with them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your loss comes about because of a business or professional relationship between you and the injured person (for example as their employer or business partner)
      `workers' compensation covers it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an amount has been, or could be, recovered for this loss under a workers' compensation law, or under a law giving effect to an international agreement
      `defect not there at supply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manufacturer has established that the defect did not exist when the goods were supplied by their actual manufacturer
      `only because of a mandatory standard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manufacturer has established that the goods had the defect only because they complied with a mandatory standard
      `undiscoverable then` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manufacturer has established that scientific or technical knowledge when it supplied the goods could not have discovered the defect
      `only the other goods' design or instructions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the goods were a component and the manufacturer has established that the defect is due only to the other goods' design, markings, instructions or warnings
GIVETH A `Defective goods answer`
`Is the manufacturer liable for my loss from another's injury question` `it supplied the goods in trade or commerce` `the goods have a safety defect` `another person was injured because of the defect` `I suffered loss from their injuries or death` `my loss comes from a business relationship with them` `workers' compensation covers it` `defect not there at supply` `only because of a mandatory standard` `undiscoverable then` `only the other goods' design or instructions` MEANS
    `the defective goods outcome`
        (`it supplied the goods in trade or commerce` AND `the goods have a safety defect` AND `another person was injured because of the defect` AND `I suffered loss from their injuries or death` AND (NOT `my loss comes from a business relationship with them`))
        `workers' compensation covers it` `defect not there at supply` `only because of a mandatory standard` `undiscoverable then` `only the other goods' design or instructions`

-- s 140(1): liable if "(a) ... supplies the goods in trade or commerce; and (b) the goods have a safety defect; and (c)
-- other goods of a kind ordinarily acquired for personal, domestic or household use or consumption are destroyed or
-- damaged because of the safety defect; and (d) the person used or consumed, or intended to use or consume, the destroyed
-- or damaged goods for personal, domestic or household use or consumption; and (e) the person suffers loss or damage as a
-- result of the destruction or damage."
@export Goods with a safety defect destroyed or damaged my other household goods: is the manufacturer liable to compensate me (Australian Consumer Law ss 140, 142, 146)? It is if it supplied the defective goods in trade or commerce, they have a safety defect, and because of it other goods of a kind ordinarily bought for personal, domestic or household use were destroyed or damaged, which you used (or meant to use) that way, and you suffered loss as a result, unless workers' compensation covers it or the manufacturer establishes a defence.
GIVEN `it supplied the goods in trade or commerce` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manufacturer supplied the defective goods in trade or commerce
      `the goods have a safety defect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defective goods' safety is not such as people generally are entitled to expect (a court's judgment)
      `my other goods were destroyed or damaged by the defect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether other goods were destroyed or damaged because of the safety defect
      `they are of a household kind` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those other goods are of a kind ordinarily bought for personal, domestic or household use or consumption
      `I used them for household purposes` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you used or consumed them, or meant to, for personal, domestic or household purposes
      `I suffered loss from it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you suffered loss or damage as a result of the destruction or damage
      `workers' compensation covers it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an amount has been, or could be, recovered for this loss under a workers' compensation law, or a law giving effect to an international agreement
      `defect not there at supply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manufacturer has established that the defect did not exist when the goods were supplied by their actual manufacturer
      `only because of a mandatory standard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manufacturer has established that the goods had the defect only because they complied with a mandatory standard
      `undiscoverable then` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manufacturer has established that scientific or technical knowledge when it supplied the goods could not have discovered the defect
      `only the other goods' design or instructions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the goods were a component and the manufacturer has established that the defect is due only to the other goods' design, markings, instructions or warnings
GIVETH A `Defective goods answer`
`Is the manufacturer liable for my damaged household goods question` `it supplied the goods in trade or commerce` `the goods have a safety defect` `my other goods were destroyed or damaged by the defect` `they are of a household kind` `I used them for household purposes` `I suffered loss from it` `workers' compensation covers it` `defect not there at supply` `only because of a mandatory standard` `undiscoverable then` `only the other goods' design or instructions` MEANS
    `the defective goods outcome`
        (`it supplied the goods in trade or commerce` AND `the goods have a safety defect` AND `my other goods were destroyed or damaged by the defect` AND `they are of a household kind` AND `I used them for household purposes` AND `I suffered loss from it`)
        `workers' compensation covers it` `defect not there at supply` `only because of a mandatory standard` `undiscoverable then` `only the other goods' design or instructions`

-- s 141(1): liable if "(a) ... supplies the goods in trade or commerce; and (b) the goods have a safety defect; and (c)
-- land, buildings or fixtures are destroyed or damaged because of the safety defect; and (d) the land, buildings or
-- fixtures are ordinarily acquired for private use; and (e) the person used, or intended to use, the land, buildings or
-- fixtures for private use; and (f) the person suffers loss or damage as a result of the destruction or damage."
@export Goods with a safety defect destroyed or damaged my home, a building or fixtures: is the manufacturer liable to compensate me (Australian Consumer Law ss 141, 142, 146)? It is if it supplied the goods in trade or commerce, they have a safety defect, and because of it land, buildings or fixtures of a kind ordinarily acquired for private use were destroyed or damaged, which you used (or meant to use) privately, and you suffered loss as a result, unless workers' compensation covers it or the manufacturer establishes a defence.
GIVEN `it supplied the goods in trade or commerce` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manufacturer supplied the defective goods in trade or commerce
      `the goods have a safety defect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the goods' safety is not such as people generally are entitled to expect (a court's judgment)
      `property was destroyed or damaged by the defect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether land, buildings or fixtures were destroyed or damaged because of the safety defect
      `it is of a private kind` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that land, building or fixture is of a kind ordinarily acquired for private use (such as a home)
      `I used it privately` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you used it, or meant to use it, for private use
      `I suffered loss from it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you suffered loss or damage as a result of the destruction or damage
      `workers' compensation covers it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an amount has been, or could be, recovered for this loss under a workers' compensation law, or a law giving effect to an international agreement
      `defect not there at supply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manufacturer has established that the defect did not exist when the goods were supplied by their actual manufacturer
      `only because of a mandatory standard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manufacturer has established that the goods had the defect only because they complied with a mandatory standard
      `undiscoverable then` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manufacturer has established that scientific or technical knowledge when it supplied the goods could not have discovered the defect
      `only the other goods' design or instructions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the goods were a component and the manufacturer has established that the defect is due only to the other goods' design, markings, instructions or warnings
GIVETH A `Defective goods answer`
`Is the manufacturer liable for my damaged home or fixtures question` `it supplied the goods in trade or commerce` `the goods have a safety defect` `property was destroyed or damaged by the defect` `it is of a private kind` `I used it privately` `I suffered loss from it` `workers' compensation covers it` `defect not there at supply` `only because of a mandatory standard` `undiscoverable then` `only the other goods' design or instructions` MEANS
    `the defective goods outcome`
        (`it supplied the goods in trade or commerce` AND `the goods have a safety defect` AND `property was destroyed or damaged by the defect` AND `it is of a private kind` AND `I used it privately` AND `I suffered loss from it`)
        `workers' compensation covers it` `defect not there at supply` `only because of a mandatory standard` `undiscoverable then` `only the other goods' design or instructions`

-- Act s 137A(1): if the loss in a s 138 or 139 action "was caused by both: (a) an act or omission of: (i) the individual
-- who suffers the injuries ...; or (ii) a person for whom that individual is responsible; and (b) a safety defect ...
-- the amount of the loss or damage is to be reduced to such extent (which may be to nil) as the court thinks fit having
-- regard to that individual's share in the responsibility". (2): the same for ss 140, 141, with the claimant's own act
-- or omission (or of a person for whom the claimant is responsible). The extent is the court's: an input, as a percentage.
@export How much of my defective goods loss can I recover if I (or the injured person) partly caused it (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 s 137A)? If the loss was caused both by the safety defect and by an act or omission of the injured person (for injury claims) or of you (for property claims), or of someone they or you are responsible for, the amount is reduced by as much as the court thinks fit, possibly to nil. Otherwise the full amount.
GIVEN `the loss` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of the loss or damage in dollars, before any reduction
      `partly caused by our own act or omission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the loss was caused partly by an act or omission of the injured person (injury claims under s 138 or 139) or of you (property claims under s 140 or 141), or of a person they or you are responsible for
      `the court's reduction` IS A NUMBER @desc The reduction the court thinks fit for that share of responsibility, as a percentage from 0 to 100 (a court's judgment)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`How much can I recover after my share is taken off question` `the loss` `partly caused by our own act or omission` `the court's reduction` MEANS
    IF (NOT `partly caused by our own act or omission`)
    THEN JUST `the loss`
    ELSE IF `the court's reduction` AT LEAST 0 AND `the court's reduction` AT MOST 100
         THEN JUST (`the loss` MINUS (`the loss` TIMES `the court's reduction` DIVIDED BY 100))
         ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Defective goods actions: time, the unknown manufacturer, and the court (ACL Part 3-5 Division 2)`

-- s 143(1): "Subject to subsection (2), a person may commence a defective goods action at any time within 3 years after
-- the time the person became aware, or ought reasonably to have become aware, of all of the following: (a) the alleged
-- loss or damage; (b) the safety defect of the goods; (c) the identity of the person who manufactured the goods."
-- (2): "A defective goods action must be commenced within 10 years of the supply by the manufacturer of the goods".
GIVEN `aware of all three on` IS A DATE, `supplied by the manufacturer on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 143 -- the last day for a defective goods action` `aware of all three on` `supplied by the manufacturer on` MEANS
    `the earlier of` (`the last day of` 3 `years after` `aware of all three on`) `and` (`the last day of` 10 `years after` `supplied by the manufacturer on`)

@export By when must I start my defective goods action against the manufacturer (Australian Consumer Law s 143)? Within 3 years after you became aware, or ought reasonably to have become aware, of the loss, the safety defect and who made the goods; and in any case within 10 years of the manufacturer's supply of the goods. The earlier of the two last days is given. For personal injury, Part VIB of the Act also applies (3 years from the date of discoverability, 12-year long stop). A last day on a weekend or public holiday is not moved here (Acts Interpretation Act 1901 s 36(2) may allow the next working day).
GIVEN `aware of all three on` IS A DATE @desc The day you became aware, or ought reasonably to have become aware, of all three: the loss or damage, the safety defect, and who manufactured the goods
      `supplied by the manufacturer on` IS A DATE @desc The day the manufacturer supplied the goods (put into circulation)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I start my defective goods action question` `aware of all three on` `supplied by the manufacturer on` MEANS
    `s 143 -- the last day for a defective goods action` `aware of all three on` `supplied by the manufacturer on`

-- Periods ruling (a): outside a period the text fixes, JUST FALSE.
@export Am I still in time to start my defective goods action (Australian Consumer Law s 143)? Yes if you start it no later than 3 years after you became (or ought reasonably to have become) aware of the loss, the defect and the manufacturer, and no later than 10 years after the manufacturer supplied the goods.
GIVEN `aware of all three on` IS A DATE @desc The day you became aware, or ought reasonably to have become aware, of the loss or damage, the safety defect and who manufactured the goods
      `supplied by the manufacturer on` IS A DATE @desc The day the manufacturer supplied the goods
      `I start it on` IS A DATE @desc The day you start (or started) the action
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I in time for my defective goods action question` `aware of all three on` `supplied by the manufacturer on` `I start it on` MEANS
    IF `the day` `I start it on` `is on or before` (`s 143 -- the last day for a defective goods action` `aware of all three on` `supplied by the manufacturer on`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 147(1): a person who "wishes to institute a defective goods action; but ... does not know who is the manufacturer
-- ... may, by written notice given to a supplier, or each supplier, of the goods who is known to the person, request the
-- supplier or suppliers to give the person particulars identifying the manufacturer of the goods, or the supplier of the
-- goods to the supplier requested." (2): "If, 30 days after the person made the request or requests, the person still
-- does not know who is the manufacturer of the goods, then each supplier: (a) to whom the request was made; and (b) who
-- did not comply with the request; is taken, for the purposes of the defective goods liability action (but not for the
-- purposes of section 142(c)), to be the manufacturer of the goods."
-- The supplier's 30 days as a regulative rule, counted in days from my request.
DECLARE `Claim party` IS ONE OF `I, the claimant`, `the supplier`
DECLARE `Claim step` IS ONE OF
    `give me particulars identifying the manufacturer, or the supplier's own supplier`

GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim step`
`s 147 -- the supplier must name the manufacturer within 30 days` MEANS
    PARTY `the supplier`
    MUST `give me particulars identifying the manufacturer, or the supplier's own supplier`
    WITHIN 30
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the supplier` BECAUSE "no particulars within 30 days after the written request: if I still do not know the manufacturer, the supplier is taken to be the manufacturer for my defective goods action (s 147(2))"

GIVEN `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 147 -- particulars on this day are in time` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 147 -- the supplier must name the manufacturer within 30 days` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the supplier` `give me particulars identifying the manufacturer, or the supplier's own supplier` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Unknown manufacturer answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the supplier is taken to be the manufacturer for my defective goods action (but not for the s 142(c) defence)`
    `no: the supplier gave me the particulars within 30 days`
    `no: I knew who the manufacturer was 30 days after my request`
    `not yet: 30 days have not passed since my request`
    `no: s 147 works only on a written request to a supplier I know of`

@export I do not know who made the goods: is the supplier I asked taken to be the manufacturer (Australian Consumer Law s 147)? You may ask, in writing, each supplier you know of to identify the manufacturer (or the supplier's own supplier). If 30 days after your request you still do not know who the manufacturer is, a supplier who did not comply is taken to be the manufacturer for your defective goods action (but it can still rely on the state-of-knowledge defence of the real manufacturer's time, s 142(c)). This reads "did not comply" as not complying within the 30 days.
GIVEN `I asked in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the supplier a written notice asking it to identify the manufacturer or its own supplier
      `asked on` IS A DATE @desc The day you made the written request
      `the supplier answered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the supplier has given you particulars identifying the manufacturer or its own supplier
      `answered on` IS A DATE @desc The day the supplier gave you the particulars (if it has not, any date; it is not used)
      `I knew the manufacturer by day 30` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, 30 days after your request, you knew who the manufacturer was (from any source)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Unknown manufacturer answer`
`Is the supplier taken to be the manufacturer question` `I asked in writing` `asked on` `the supplier answered` `answered on` `I knew the manufacturer by day 30` `today` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I asked in writing`)
    THEN `no: s 147 works only on a written request to a supplier I know of`
    ELSE IF `the supplier answered` AND `s 147 -- particulars on this day are in time` (`the days from` `asked on` `to` `answered on`)
         THEN `no: the supplier gave me the particulars within 30 days`
         ELSE IF `the days from` `asked on` `to` `today` AT MOST 30
              THEN `not yet: 30 days have not passed since my request`
              ELSE IF `I knew the manufacturer by day 30`
                   THEN `no: I knew who the manufacturer was 30 days after my request`
                   ELSE `yes: the supplier is taken to be the manufacturer for my defective goods action (but not for the s 142(c) defence)`

-- s 144: "If 2 or more persons are liable under Division 1 for the same loss or damage, they are jointly and severally
-- liable." JUST TRUE where two or more are liable; the text is silent otherwise (NOTHING).
@export Can I recover the whole of my loss from any one of the manufacturers liable for it (Australian Consumer Law s 144)? Yes where two or more are liable under Part 3-5 for the same loss or damage: they are jointly and severally liable.
GIVEN `how many are liable for the same loss` IS A NUMBER @desc How many persons (manufacturers, deemed manufacturers or importers) are liable to you under Part 3-5 for the same loss or damage
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I recover the whole loss from any one of them question` `how many are liable for the same loss` MEANS
    IF `how many are liable for the same loss` AT LEAST 2
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 148(1)-(3): where the defendant raises the defence that the goods had the defect only because of compliance with a
-- Commonwealth mandatory standard, it must give the Commonwealth notice, which "makes the Commonwealth a defendant"; and
-- if the court finds I "would, but for the defence ..., have succeeded" against the defendant, "the Commonwealth, and not
-- the person ... is liable to pay the plaintiff for the amount of the loss or damage caused by the safety defect".
-- JUST TRUE where both hold; NOTHING otherwise (s 148 says nothing of other cases).
@export The manufacturer says the goods were defective only because they met a Commonwealth mandatory standard: does the Commonwealth pay me instead (Australian Consumer Law s 148)? Yes if the defendant raises that defence (it must then notify the Commonwealth, which becomes a defendant) and the court finds you would have won against the defendant but for that defence. The Commonwealth, not the defendant, then pays the loss caused by the safety defect.
GIVEN `it raised the Commonwealth standard defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant raised the defence that the goods had the defect only because they complied with a mandatory standard imposed by a Commonwealth law
      `I would have won but for that defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that you would have succeeded against the defendant but for that defence
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the Commonwealth pay me instead question` `it raised the Commonwealth standard defence` `I would have won but for that defence` MEANS
    IF `it raised the Commonwealth standard defence` AND `I would have won but for that defence`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 149(2): "The regulator may only make the application if it has obtained the written consent of the person, or each
-- of the persons, on whose behalf the application is being made." "only": JUST FALSE without my consent.
@export Can the regulator (the ACCC or a State or Territory consumer agency) bring a defective goods action on my behalf (Australian Consumer Law s 149)? Only with your written consent.
GIVEN `I gave written consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have given the regulator your written consent to bring the action on your behalf
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the regulator sue for me question` `I gave written consent` MEANS
    IF `I gave written consent`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 150(1): a contract term "that purports to exclude, restrict or modify, or has the effect of excluding, restricting or
-- modifying" the application of Part 3-5, the exercise of a right under it, or any liability under it, "is void". (2):
-- "A term ... is not taken to exclude, restrict or modify the application of a provision of this Part unless the term
-- does so expressly or is inconsistent with that provision."
@export Can a contract term take away my rights against the manufacturer for defective goods (Australian Consumer Law s 150)? No: a term that expressly excludes, restricts or modifies Part 3-5, a right under it, or liability under it, or is inconsistent with it, is void. A term that does neither is not treated as doing so.
GIVEN `the term expressly limits Part 3-5` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the contract term expressly excludes, restricts or modifies the manufacturer's liability for safety defects, or your right to claim for them
      `the term is inconsistent with Part 3-5` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the term is inconsistent with the manufacturer-liability provisions (for example, it would defeat or cap a claim they give you)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the term limiting the manufacturer's liability void question` `the term expressly limits Part 3-5` `the term is inconsistent with Part 3-5` MEANS
    IF `the term expressly limits Part 3-5` OR `the term is inconsistent with Part 3-5`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Damages and compensation orders for a contravention (ACL ss 236, 237; the Act's ss 137B, 137C, 137E)`

DECLARE `Part of the Australian Consumer Law` IS ONE OF
    `Part 2-1 (misleading or deceptive conduct)`
    `Part 2-2 (unconscionable conduct)`
    `Part 2-3 (unfair contract terms)`
    `Part 3-1 (unfair practices)`
    `Part 3-2 (consumer transactions)`
    `Part 3-3 (safety of consumer goods and product related services)`
    `Part 3-4 (information standards)`
    `Part 3-5 (liability of manufacturers for goods with safety defects)`
    `Part 5-4 Division 2 (action for damages against manufacturers for consumer guarantees)`
    `another provision`

GIVEN p IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a Part 2-1 or 3-1 contravention` p IF
    CONSIDER p
    WHEN `Part 2-1 (misleading or deceptive conduct)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Part 3-1 (unfair practices)` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN p IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a Chapter 2 or 3 provision` p IF
    CONSIDER p
    WHEN `Part 2-1 (misleading or deceptive conduct)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Part 2-2 (unconscionable conduct)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Part 2-3 (unfair contract terms)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Part 3-1 (unfair practices)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Part 3-2 (consumer transactions)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Part 3-3 (safety of consumer goods and product related services)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Part 3-4 (information standards)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Part 3-5 (liability of manufacturers for goods with safety defects)` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- ACL s 236(1): "If: (a) a person (the claimant) suffers loss or damage because of the conduct of another person; and
-- (b) the conduct contravened a provision of Chapter 2 or 3; the claimant may recover the amount of the loss or damage by
-- action against that other person, or against any person involved in the contravention."
-- Act s 137C(1): "A person is not entitled to recover an amount of loss or damage by action under subsection 236(1) ...
-- to the extent to which: (a) the action would be based on the conduct contravening a provision of Part 2-1 or 3-1 ...;
-- and (b) the loss or damage is, or results from, death or personal injury; and (c) the death or personal injury does
-- not result from smoking or other use of tobacco products."
DECLARE `Damages answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I may recover the amount of my loss or damage by action against them`
    `no: a condition of s 236(1) is not met`
    `no: personal injury or death loss cannot be recovered for a Part 2-1 or 3-1 contravention, except from tobacco (s 137C)`

@export Can I sue for the loss a breach of the Australian Consumer Law caused me (Australian Consumer Law s 236; Competition and Consumer Act 2010 s 137C)? You may recover the amount of your loss or damage from the person whose conduct contravened Chapter 2 or 3 (for example misleading conduct, unconscionable conduct, unfair practices, consumer transactions, product safety), or from anyone involved in the contravention. But loss that is or results from death or personal injury cannot be recovered under s 236 for misleading conduct (Part 2-1) or unfair practices (Part 3-1), unless it results from smoking or other tobacco use. The court may reduce economic or property loss for a s 18 claim where you failed to take reasonable care (s 137B), and Part VIB limits personal injury damages for other Parts.
GIVEN `the Part contravened` IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law` @desc Which Part of the Australian Consumer Law the conduct contravened. One of: Part 2-1 (misleading or deceptive conduct), Part 2-2 (unconscionable conduct), Part 2-3 (unfair contract terms), Part 3-1 (unfair practices), Part 3-2 (consumer transactions), Part 3-3 (safety of consumer goods and product related services), Part 3-4 (information standards), Part 3-5, Part 5-4 Division 2, another provision
      `I suffered loss because of the conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you suffered loss or damage because of the other person's conduct
      `I sue the contravener or someone involved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you sue engaged in the conduct, or was involved in the contravention (aided, induced, was knowingly concerned in or conspired in it)
      `the loss is death or personal injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the loss or damage is, or results from, death or personal injury
      `it results from tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death or personal injury results from smoking or other use of tobacco products
GIVETH A `Damages answer`
`Can I recover my loss for the contravention question` `the Part contravened` `I suffered loss because of the conduct` `I sue the contravener or someone involved` `the loss is death or personal injury` `it results from tobacco` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`a Chapter 2 or 3 provision` `the Part contravened` AND `I suffered loss because of the conduct` AND `I sue the contravener or someone involved`))
    THEN `no: a condition of s 236(1) is not met`
    ELSE IF `a Part 2-1 or 3-1 contravention` `the Part contravened` AND `the loss is death or personal injury` AND (NOT `it results from tobacco`)
         THEN `no: personal injury or death loss cannot be recovered for a Part 2-1 or 3-1 contravention, except from tobacco (s 137C)`
         ELSE `yes: I may recover the amount of my loss or damage by action against them`

-- ACL s 236(2): "An action under subsection (1) may be commenced at any time within 6 years after the day on which the
-- cause of action that relates to the conduct accrued." When the cause of action accrued is a question of law: an input.
@export By when must I start my action for damages for a breach of the Australian Consumer Law (Australian Consumer Law s 236(2))? Within 6 years after the day the cause of action accrued (usually when you first suffered loss). For personal injury, Part VIB's time limits also apply where it reaches the claim. A last day on a weekend or public holiday is not moved here (Acts Interpretation Act 1901 s 36(2) may allow the next working day).
GIVEN `the cause of action accrued on` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued (for a damages claim, usually the day you first suffered loss or damage)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I start my damages action question` `the cause of action accrued on` MEANS
    `the last day of` 6 `years after` `the cause of action accrued on`

-- ACL s 237(1): a court may, "on application of a person (the injured person) who has suffered, or is likely to suffer,
-- loss or damage because another person has engaged in conduct in contravention of a provision of Chapter 2, 3 or 4",
-- make orders against the person who engaged in the conduct or a person involved. (3): "An application ... may be made at
-- any time before the end of 6 years from the day on which the cause of action accrued". Act s 137E(1): a court "must not
-- make an order under subsection 237(1) ... to compensate a person" for loss that is or results from death or personal
-- injury, where the action would be based on a Part 2-1 or 3-1 contravention and it does not result from tobacco.
-- Period ruling (a): JUST FALSE out of time. s 137E: JUST FALSE (flat "must not"). A Chapter 4 offence provision is
-- outside the Parts listed here: "another provision" answers NOTHING.
@export Can I apply to the court for a compensation order for a breach of the Australian Consumer Law, and am I in time (Australian Consumer Law s 237; Competition and Consumer Act 2010 s 137E)? You may apply if you have suffered, or are likely to suffer, loss because someone contravened Chapter 2 or 3 (or a Chapter 4 offence), before the end of 6 years from the day the cause of action accrued. The court cannot order compensation for death or personal injury caused by misleading conduct (Part 2-1) or unfair practices (Part 3-1), unless it results from tobacco use.
GIVEN `the Part contravened` IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law` @desc Which Part of the Australian Consumer Law the conduct contravened. One of: Part 2-1, Part 2-2, Part 2-3, Part 3-1, Part 3-2, Part 3-3, Part 3-4, Part 3-5, Part 5-4 Division 2, another provision
      `I suffered or am likely to suffer loss` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have suffered, or are likely to suffer, loss or damage because of the conduct
      `the loss is death or personal injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the loss or damage is, or results from, death or personal injury
      `it results from tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death or personal injury results from smoking or other use of tobacco products
      `the cause of action accrued on` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued
      `I apply on` IS A DATE @desc The day you make (or made) the application
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply for a compensation order question` `the Part contravened` `I suffered or am likely to suffer loss` `the loss is death or personal injury` `it results from tobacco` `the cause of action accrued on` `I apply on` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`a Chapter 2 or 3 provision` `the Part contravened`))
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF (NOT `I suffered or am likely to suffer loss`)
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF `a Part 2-1 or 3-1 contravention` `the Part contravened` AND `the loss is death or personal injury` AND (NOT `it results from tobacco`)
              THEN JUST FALSE
              ELSE IF `the day` `I apply on` `is on or before` (`the last day of` 6 `years after` `the cause of action accrued on`)
                   THEN JUST TRUE
                   ELSE JUST FALSE

-- Act s 137B: where "(a) a person (the claimant) makes a claim under subsection 236(1) ... in relation to economic loss,
-- or damage to property ...; and (b) the conduct contravened section 18 ...; and (c) the claimant suffered the loss or
-- damage as result: (i) partly of the claimant's failure to take reasonable care; and (ii) partly of the conduct of the
-- other person; and (d) the other person did not intend to cause the loss or damage and did not fraudulently cause the
-- loss or damage; the amount ... is to be reduced to the extent to which a court thinks just and equitable having regard
-- to the claimant's share in the responsibility". The extent is the court's: an input.
@export How much of my loss from misleading conduct can I recover if I partly failed to take reasonable care (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 s 137B)? For a s 236 claim for economic loss or property damage caused by misleading or deceptive conduct (s 18), where the loss came partly from your own failure to take reasonable care and the other side neither intended the loss nor caused it by fraud, the amount is reduced as far as the court thinks just and equitable. Otherwise the full amount.
GIVEN `the loss` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of your loss or damage in dollars, before any reduction
      `economic loss or property damage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is for economic loss or damage to property (not personal injury)
      `the conduct contravened s 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct was misleading or deceptive conduct contravening s 18
      `partly my failure to take reasonable care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you suffered the loss partly because you failed to take reasonable care
      `they intended it or acted fraudulently` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other person intended to cause the loss or caused it fraudulently
      `the court's reduction` IS A NUMBER @desc The reduction the court thinks just and equitable for your share of responsibility, as a percentage from 0 to 100 (a court's judgment)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`How much can I recover for misleading conduct after my share question` `the loss` `economic loss or property damage` `the conduct contravened s 18` `partly my failure to take reasonable care` `they intended it or acted fraudulently` `the court's reduction` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`economic loss or property damage` AND `the conduct contravened s 18` AND `partly my failure to take reasonable care` AND (NOT `they intended it or acted fraudulently`)))
    THEN JUST `the loss`
    ELSE IF `the court's reduction` AT LEAST 0 AND `the court's reduction` AT MOST 100
         THEN JUST (`the loss` MINUS (`the loss` TIMES `the court's reduction` DIVIDED BY 100))
         ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Personal injury and death claims: does Part VIB reach my claim (Part VIB Division 1; ss 137C, 137E)`

DECLARE `Part VIB reach` IS ONE OF
    `Part VIB applies in full: its time limits and its limits on damages`
    `only Part VIB's time limits (Division 2) and structured settlements (Division 7) apply: the claim is about tobacco`
    `no personal injury damages can be awarded at all for this contravention (s 137C or s 137E)`
    `Part VIB does not apply to this claim`

-- s 87E(1): "This Part applies to proceedings taken under the Australian Consumer Law: (a) that relate to Part 2-2, 3-3,
-- 3-4 or 3-5, or Division 2 of Part 5-4 ...; and (b) in which the plaintiff is seeking an award of personal injury
-- damages; and (c) that are not proceedings in respect of the death of or personal injury to a person resulting from
-- smoking or other use of tobacco products." (2): "However, for the purposes of Divisions 2 and 7, paragraph (1)(c) does
-- not apply." s 137C(2) and s 137E(2), (3) apply Divisions 2 and 7 to tobacco claims for Part 2-1 or 3-1
-- contraventions; s 137C(1) and s 137E(1) bar non-tobacco personal injury recovery for those Parts.
GIVEN p IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a Part listed in s 87E(1)(a)` p IF
    CONSIDER p
    WHEN `Part 2-2 (unconscionable conduct)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Part 3-3 (safety of consumer goods and product related services)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Part 3-4 (information standards)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Part 3-5 (liability of manufacturers for goods with safety defects)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Part 5-4 Division 2 (action for damages against manufacturers for consumer guarantees)` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN p IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law`, `about tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Part VIB reach`
`the reach of Part VIB` p `about tobacco` MEANS
    IF `a Part listed in s 87E(1)(a)` p
    THEN IF `about tobacco`
         THEN `only Part VIB's time limits (Division 2) and structured settlements (Division 7) apply: the claim is about tobacco`
         ELSE `Part VIB applies in full: its time limits and its limits on damages`
    ELSE IF `a Part 2-1 or 3-1 contravention` p
         THEN IF `about tobacco`
              THEN `only Part VIB's time limits (Division 2) and structured settlements (Division 7) apply: the claim is about tobacco`
              ELSE `no personal injury damages can be awarded at all for this contravention (s 137C or s 137E)`
         ELSE `Part VIB does not apply to this claim`

@export I am claiming personal injury or death damages under the Australian Consumer Law: does Part VIB of the Competition and Consumer Act 2010 limit my claim (ss 87E, 137C, 137E)? Part VIB (time limits, caps on non-economic loss, earnings, care, interest, and no exemplary damages) applies to claims about unconscionable conduct, product safety and information standards, defective goods (Part 3-5) and consumer-guarantee actions against manufacturers. For tobacco injuries only its time limits and structured settlements apply. For misleading conduct or unfair practices, personal injury damages cannot be recovered at all, except for tobacco injuries.
GIVEN `my claim relates to` IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law` @desc Which Part of the Australian Consumer Law your claim relates to. One of: Part 2-1 (misleading or deceptive conduct), Part 2-2 (unconscionable conduct), Part 2-3 (unfair contract terms), Part 3-1 (unfair practices), Part 3-2 (consumer transactions), Part 3-3 (safety of consumer goods), Part 3-4 (information standards), Part 3-5 (defective goods), Part 5-4 Division 2 (consumer guarantee action against a manufacturer), another provision
      `I seek personal injury damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are seeking damages or compensation for death or personal injury
      `it results from tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death or injury results from smoking or other use of tobacco products
GIVETH A `Part VIB reach`
`Does Part VIB limit my claim question` `my claim relates to` `I seek personal injury damages` `it results from tobacco` MEANS
    IF `I seek personal injury damages`
    THEN `the reach of Part VIB` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco`
    ELSE `Part VIB does not apply to this claim`

GIVEN p IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law`, `about tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Part VIB applies in full` p `about tobacco` IF
    CONSIDER `the reach of Part VIB` p `about tobacco`
    WHEN `Part VIB applies in full: its time limits and its limits on damages` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN p IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law`, `about tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Part VIB's time limits apply` p `about tobacco` IF
    CONSIDER `the reach of Part VIB` p `about tobacco`
    WHEN `Part VIB applies in full: its time limits and its limits on damages` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `only Part VIB's time limits (Division 2) and structured settlements (Division 7) apply: the claim is about tobacco` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `Part VIB time limits for personal injury and death claims (Division 2)`

-- s 87G(1): the date of discoverability is "the first date when the plaintiff ... knows or ought to know" that the
-- death or injury occurred, that it "was attributable to a contravention of this Act", and that the injury "was
-- significant enough to justify bringing an action". (4) a minor: facts a capable parent or guardian knows or ought to
-- know are taken to be the plaintiff's. (5) an incapacitated person with a guardian or manager under State or Territory
-- law: facts that person knows or ought to know are taken to be the plaintiff's. So the date is the earlier of mine and
-- theirs. Each date is a court's judgment of actual or constructive knowledge: an input.
@export What is my date of discoverability for a personal injury claim (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 s 87G(1), (4), (5))? It is the first day you knew, or ought to have known (had you taken all reasonable steps), that the injury had occurred, that it was caused by a contravention, and that it was significant enough to justify suing. If you were a minor, what a capable parent or guardian knew or ought to have known counts as yours; if you were incapacitated with a guardian or estate manager, what they knew or ought to have known counts. The earlier day is your date.
GIVEN `I knew or ought to have known all three on` IS A DATE @desc The first day you knew, or ought to have known, all three: that the injury (or death) occurred, that it was caused by a contravention, and that the injury was significant enough to justify suing
      `someone else's knowledge counts` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a minor with a capable parent or guardian, or an incapacitated person with a guardian or estate manager appointed under State or Territory law, whose knowledge counts as yours
      `they knew or ought to have known all three on` IS A DATE @desc The first day that parent, guardian or manager knew, or ought to have known, all three (if no one's knowledge counts, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A DATE
`What is my date of discoverability question` `I knew or ought to have known all three on` `someone else's knowledge counts` `they knew or ought to have known all three on` MEANS
    IF `someone else's knowledge counts`
    THEN `the earlier of` `I knew or ought to have known all three on` `and` `they knew or ought to have known all three on`
    ELSE `I knew or ought to have known all three on`

-- s 87G(6): if the plaintiff sues as the personal representative of a deceased person, the date of discoverability "is
-- the earliest of: (a) if, had the deceased person commenced a proceeding ... before his or her death, the date of
-- discoverability under subsection (1) would have occurred more than 3 years before the death--that date; or (b) if, at
-- the time of the plaintiff's appointment as personal representative, the plaintiff knew, or ought to have known, all of
-- the matters referred to in paragraphs (1)(a), (b) and (c)--the date of the appointment; or (c) if the first time at
-- which the plaintiff knew, or ought to have known, all of the matters ... was after the date of appointment--the date of
-- that first time." None of the three applies only if I have not yet known: NOTHING then (no date yet).
@export I am claiming as the personal representative of someone who died: what is the date of discoverability (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 s 87G(6))? The earliest of: the deceased's own date of discoverability, if it fell more than 3 years before their death; the day you were appointed, if you then knew or ought to have known that the death or injury occurred, was caused by a contravention, and (for an injury) was significant enough to sue; or otherwise the first day after your appointment that you knew or ought to have known those things.
GIVEN `the deceased had a date of discoverability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, had the deceased sued before dying, a date of discoverability would already have arisen for them
      `the deceased's date` IS A DATE @desc That date of discoverability for the deceased (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `they died on` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `I was appointed on` IS A DATE @desc The day you were appointed personal representative (executor or administrator)
      `I knew at appointment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when appointed, you knew or ought to have known all three matters
      `I have since come to know` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you came to know (or ought to have known) all three matters after your appointment
      `I first knew on` IS A DATE @desc The first day after your appointment that you knew or ought to have known all three (if not applicable, any date)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`What is the date of discoverability for the deceased's claim question` `the deceased had a date of discoverability` `the deceased's date` `they died on` `I was appointed on` `I knew at appointment` `I have since come to know` `I first knew on` MEANS
    IF `limb (a)`
    THEN IF `limb (b) or (c)`
         THEN JUST (`the earlier of` `the deceased's date` `and` `my own date`)
         ELSE JUST `the deceased's date`
    ELSE IF `limb (b) or (c)`
         THEN JUST `my own date`
         ELSE NOTHING
    WHERE
        `limb (a)` MEANS `the deceased had a date of discoverability` AND `the day` `they died on` `is after` (`the last day of` 3 `years after` `the deceased's date`)
        `limb (b) or (c)` MEANS `I knew at appointment` OR `I have since come to know`
        `my own date` MEANS
            IF `I knew at appointment`
            THEN `I was appointed on`
            ELSE `I first knew on`

-- s 87F(1): "A court must not award personal injury damages in a proceeding to which this Part applies if the
-- proceeding was commenced: (a) after the end of the period of 3 years after the date of discoverability ...; or (b)
-- after the end of the long-stop period". (1A): (b) "does not apply" to tobacco claims. s 87H(1): the long-stop period is
-- "(a) the period of 12 years following the act or omission alleged to have caused the death or injury; or (b) that
-- period as extended by the court." (2): "The court must not extend the period by more than 3 years beyond the date of
-- discoverability". s 87J: disregard any period during which the plaintiff was a minor not in the custody of a capable
-- parent or guardian, or an incapacitated person with no guardian or manager. s 87K: where the victim was a minor at the
-- time and the defendant was then a parent or guardian, or in a close relationship with one, disregard any period
-- "before the victim turns 25" or, if the victim died before 25, "before the victim's death".
-- Reading taken (NOTES.md, forks F3-F5): a disregarded s 87K stretch moves the start of each count to the day before the
-- victim's 25th birthday (or death), so counting starts on that day; s 87J days, given as a number not already inside the
-- s 87K stretch, are added to each last day; an extension is capped at the last day of 3 years after the date of
-- discoverability.
GIVEN `the count starts after` IS A DATE, n IS A NUMBER, `close relationship rule` IS A BOOLEAN, `turns 25 or died on` IS A DATE, `days disregarded` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the Part VIB last day` `the count starts after` n `close relationship rule` `turns 25 or died on` `days disregarded` MEANS
    `the date` `days disregarded` `days after` (`the last day of` n `years after` `the start`)
    WHERE
        `the start` MEANS
            IF `close relationship rule`
            THEN `the later of` `the count starts after` `and` (`the date` (0 MINUS 1) `days after` `turns 25 or died on`)
            ELSE `the count starts after`

GIVEN `date of discoverability` IS A DATE, `act or omission on` IS A DATE, `about tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN
      `close relationship rule` IS A BOOLEAN, `turns 25 or died on` IS A DATE, `days disregarded` IS A NUMBER
      `the court extended the long stop` IS A BOOLEAN, `extended to` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 87F -- the last day to commence` `date of discoverability` `act or omission on` `about tobacco` `close relationship rule` `turns 25 or died on` `days disregarded` `the court extended the long stop` `extended to` MEANS
    IF `about tobacco`
    THEN `the basic last day`
    ELSE `the earlier of` `the basic last day` `and` `the long-stop last day`
    WHERE
        `the basic last day` MEANS `the Part VIB last day` `date of discoverability` 3 `close relationship rule` `turns 25 or died on` `days disregarded`
        `the twelve-year last day` MEANS `the Part VIB last day` `act or omission on` 12 `close relationship rule` `turns 25 or died on` `days disregarded`
        `the extension cap` MEANS `the last day of` 3 `years after` `date of discoverability`
        `the long-stop last day` MEANS
            IF `the court extended the long stop`
            THEN `the later of` `the twelve-year last day` `and` (`the earlier of` `extended to` `and` `the extension cap`)
            ELSE `the twelve-year last day`

@export By when must I start my personal injury or death claim under Part VIB (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 ss 87F, 87H, 87J, 87K)? The court must not award personal injury damages if the claim is started more than 3 years after the date of discoverability, or after the 12-year long stop from the act or omission that caused the injury (the long stop does not apply to tobacco claims). The court may extend the long stop, but not beyond 3 years after the date of discoverability. Time while you were a minor with no capable parent or guardian, or incapacitated with no guardian or manager, is not counted; and if you were a child hurt by a parent or guardian (or someone close to them), nothing before you turn 25 counts. A last day on a weekend or public holiday is not moved here.
GIVEN `my date of discoverability` IS A DATE @desc Your date of discoverability (the first day you knew or ought to have known of the injury, that a contravention caused it, and that it was significant enough to sue)
      `the act or omission happened on` IS A DATE @desc The day of the act or omission alleged to have caused the death or injury
      `it results from tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death or injury results from smoking or other use of tobacco products
      `I was a child hurt by a parent or someone close to them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were under 18 at the time of the act or omission and the person you are suing was then your parent or guardian, or someone close enough to them to discourage a claim or your telling them
      `I turn 25 on` IS A DATE @desc Your 25th birthday (or, for a claim about a victim who died before turning 25, the day of their death); if the rule above does not apply, any date
      `days not counted` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days you were a minor not in the custody of a capable parent or guardian, or incapacitated with no guardian or estate manager (count only days not already before your 25th birthday under the rule above)
      `the court extended the long stop` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has extended the 12-year long-stop period
      `extended to` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the long-stop period as the court extended it (if not extended, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I start my personal injury claim question` `my date of discoverability` `the act or omission happened on` `it results from tobacco` `I was a child hurt by a parent or someone close to them` `I turn 25 on` `days not counted` `the court extended the long stop` `extended to` MEANS
    `s 87F -- the last day to commence` `my date of discoverability` `the act or omission happened on` `it results from tobacco` `I was a child hurt by a parent or someone close to them` `I turn 25 on` `days not counted` `the court extended the long stop` `extended to`

-- "A court must not award ... if the proceeding was commenced ... after the end of" the period: JUST TRUE (barred) after
-- the last day, JUST FALSE (this Division does not bar it) on or before; NOTHING where Division 2 does not reach the claim.
@export Is my personal injury or death claim out of time under Part VIB (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 ss 87E, 87F, 87H, 87J, 87K, 137C, 137E)? If Part VIB's time limits reach your claim, the court must not award personal injury damages if the claim was started after the last day: 3 years after your date of discoverability, or the 12-year long stop (not for tobacco claims), with the periods not counted while you were an unprotected minor or incapacitated, or (for a child hurt by a parent or someone close to them) before you turn 25. Yes means barred.
GIVEN `my claim relates to` IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law` @desc Which Part of the Australian Consumer Law your claim relates to. One of: Part 2-1, Part 2-2, Part 2-3, Part 3-1, Part 3-2, Part 3-3, Part 3-4, Part 3-5, Part 5-4 Division 2, another provision
      `it results from tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death or injury results from smoking or other use of tobacco products
      `my date of discoverability` IS A DATE @desc Your date of discoverability
      `the act or omission happened on` IS A DATE @desc The day of the act or omission alleged to have caused the death or injury
      `I was a child hurt by a parent or someone close to them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were under 18 at the time and the person you are suing was then your parent or guardian, or someone close to them
      `I turn 25 on` IS A DATE @desc Your 25th birthday (or the victim's death, if they died before 25); if not applicable, any date
      `days not counted` IS A NUMBER @desc Days you were a minor without a capable parent or guardian, or incapacitated without a guardian or manager (not already before your 25th birthday under the rule above)
      `the court extended the long stop` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has extended the 12-year long-stop period
      `extended to` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the long-stop period as extended (if not extended, any date)
      `I started it on` IS A DATE @desc The day you started (or will start) the proceeding
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my personal injury claim out of time question` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco` `my date of discoverability` `the act or omission happened on` `I was a child hurt by a parent or someone close to them` `I turn 25 on` `days not counted` `the court extended the long stop` `extended to` `I started it on` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`Part VIB's time limits apply` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco`))
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `the day` `I started it on` `is after` (`s 87F -- the last day to commence` `my date of discoverability` `the act or omission happened on` `it results from tobacco` `I was a child hurt by a parent or someone close to them` `I turn 25 on` `days not counted` `the court extended the long stop` `extended to`)
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Part VIB limits on damages for non-economic loss (Division 3)`

-- s 87M(1): the maximum amount of damages for non-economic loss is "(a) during the year in which this Part commences--
-- $250,000; or (b) during a later year--the amount worked out (to the nearest multiple of $10) as follows:" Previous
-- maximum amount x (Current September CPI number / Previous September CPI number) (formula from the formulas sidecar).
-- (2): "If an amount worked out under paragraph (1)(b) is a multiple of $5 (but not a multiple of $10), round the amount
-- up to the nearest multiple of $10." Part VIB commenced on 13 July 2004 (endnote: Trade Practices Amendment (Personal
-- Injuries and Death) Act (No. 2) 2004, commencement 13 July 2004 (s 2)); "year" read as the calendar year (fork F7).
-- The CPI numbers (s 87N) are published by the Australian Statistician and not deposited: inputs.
GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`to the nearest multiple of $10, a $5 rounded up` x MEANS FLOOR ((x DIVIDED BY 10) PLUS 0.5) TIMES 10

@export What is the maximum amount of damages for non-economic loss for a year (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 s 87M)? In 2004, when Part VIB commenced, it was $250,000. For each later year it is the previous year's maximum multiplied by the September-quarter CPI (all groups, eight capitals) of the year before, divided by the September-quarter CPI of the year before that, rounded to the nearest $10 (an amount ending in $5 rounds up).
GIVEN `the year` IS A NUMBER @desc The calendar year you want the maximum for (2004 or later)
      `the previous year's maximum` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum amount for the year before, in dollars (not used for 2004)
      `the current September CPI` IS A NUMBER @desc The All Groups CPI (weighted average of the 8 capital cities) for the September quarter of the year before the year asked about (not used for 2004)
      `the previous September CPI` IS A NUMBER @desc The same CPI for the September quarter one year earlier (not used for 2004)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the maximum for non-economic loss for the year question` `the year` `the previous year's maximum` `the current September CPI` `the previous September CPI` MEANS
    IF `the year` EQUALS 2004
    THEN JUST 250000
    ELSE IF `the year` GREATER THAN 2004 AND `the previous September CPI` GREATER THAN 0
         THEN JUST (`to the nearest multiple of $10, a $5 rounded up` (`the previous year's maximum` TIMES (`the current September CPI` DIVIDED BY `the previous September CPI`)))
         ELSE NOTHING

-- s 87R table: severity (as a proportion of a most extreme case) to damages (as a proportion of the maximum amount).
-- The table has rows for whole percentages only; a severity between two rows is not in it (NOTHING; fork F8).
GIVEN `the severity` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 87R -- the table proportion` `the severity` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the severity`
    WHEN 15 THEN JUST 0.01
    WHEN 16 THEN JUST 0.015
    WHEN 17 THEN JUST 0.02
    WHEN 18 THEN JUST 0.025
    WHEN 19 THEN JUST 0.03
    WHEN 20 THEN JUST 0.035
    WHEN 21 THEN JUST 0.04
    WHEN 22 THEN JUST 0.045
    WHEN 23 THEN JUST 0.05
    WHEN 24 THEN JUST 0.055
    WHEN 25 THEN JUST 0.065
    WHEN 26 THEN JUST 0.08
    WHEN 27 THEN JUST 0.10
    WHEN 28 THEN JUST 0.14
    WHEN 29 THEN JUST 0.18
    WHEN 30 THEN JUST 0.23
    WHEN 31 THEN JUST 0.26
    WHEN 32 THEN JUST 0.30
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- ss 87L, 87P-87S: the most the court may award for non-economic loss.
-- s 87P: the maximum only "in a most extreme case" (non-economic loss "of the gravest conceivable kind": 100%).
-- s 87Q: at least 33% but less than 100%: at most "the applicable percentage of the maximum amount".
-- s 87R: at least 15% but less than 33%: at most the table proportion of the maximum.
-- s 87S: less than 15%: "the court must not award personal injury damages for non-economic loss" (0).
GIVEN `the severity` IS A NUMBER, `the maximum` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`ss 87P-87S -- the cap on non-economic loss` `the severity` `the maximum` MEANS
    IF `the severity` LESS THAN 0 OR `the severity` GREATER THAN 100
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `the severity` EQUALS 100
         THEN JUST `the maximum`
         ELSE IF `the severity` AT LEAST 33
              THEN JUST (`the maximum` TIMES `the severity` DIVIDED BY 100)
              ELSE IF `the severity` AT LEAST 15
                   THEN CONSIDER `s 87R -- the table proportion` `the severity`
                        WHEN JUST q THEN JUST (`the maximum` TIMES q)
                        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
                   ELSE JUST 0

@export What is the most the court can award me for pain and suffering (non-economic loss) (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 ss 87L-87S)? It depends on how severe your non-economic loss is as a percentage of a most extreme case (the court's judgment): under 15%, nothing; 15% to 32%, a fixed share of the maximum amount from the s 87R table (1% of it at 15%, rising to 30% at 32%); 33% to 99%, that percentage of the maximum; the full maximum only in a most extreme case (100%). Applies only where Part VIB applies in full (not tobacco claims).
GIVEN `my claim relates to` IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law` @desc Which Part of the Australian Consumer Law your claim relates to. One of: Part 2-1, Part 2-2, Part 2-3, Part 3-1, Part 3-2, Part 3-3, Part 3-4, Part 3-5, Part 5-4 Division 2, another provision
      `it results from tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death or injury results from smoking or other use of tobacco products
      `the severity` IS A NUMBER @desc The severity of your non-economic loss (pain and suffering, loss of amenities, loss of expectation of life, disfigurement) as a percentage of a most extreme case, from 0 to 100 (the court's judgment; the s 87R table has whole percentages)
      `the maximum for the year` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum amount of damages for non-economic loss for the year of the award, in dollars (s 87M; $250,000 in 2004, indexed to CPI since)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the most for my non-economic loss question` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco` `the severity` `the maximum for the year` MEANS
    IF `Part VIB applies in full` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco`
    THEN `ss 87P-87S -- the cap on non-economic loss` `the severity` `the maximum for the year`
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Part VIB limits on damages for lost earnings, care and superannuation (Divisions 4-6)`

-- s 87U: the court "must disregard the amount by which the plaintiff's gross weekly earnings during any quarter would
-- (but for the personal injury or death in question) have exceeded" twice average weekly earnings for the quarter (or, if
-- not yet ascertainable when the award is made, for the most recent quarter that is). s 87V: average weekly earnings are
-- the Australian Statistician's all-employees total earnings, seasonally adjusted, for the quarter: not deposited, an input.
@export How much of my weekly earnings can count in damages for lost earnings or earning capacity (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 ss 87U, 87V)? The court disregards whatever your gross weekly earnings would have been above twice average weekly earnings for the quarter (or, if that quarter's figure was not yet published when the award was made, the most recent published quarter). Applies only where Part VIB applies in full.
GIVEN `my claim relates to` IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law` @desc Which Part of the Australian Consumer Law your claim relates to. One of: Part 2-1, Part 2-2, Part 2-3, Part 3-1, Part 3-2, Part 3-3, Part 3-4, Part 3-5, Part 5-4 Division 2, another provision
      `it results from tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death or injury results from smoking or other use of tobacco products
      `my weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER @desc Your gross weekly earnings in the quarter, in dollars, as they would have been but for the injury or death
      `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER @desc Average weekly earnings (all employees, total earnings, seasonally adjusted, published by the Australian Statistician) for that quarter, or for the latest published quarter if that one was not published when the award was made
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`How much of my weekly earnings counts question` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco` `my weekly earnings` `average weekly earnings` MEANS
    IF `Part VIB applies in full` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco`
    THEN JUST (min `my weekly earnings` (2 TIMES `average weekly earnings`))
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 87W(2): "The court must be satisfied that: (a) there is (or was) a reasonable need for the services to be provided;
-- and (b) the need has arisen (or arose) solely because of personal injury ...; and (c) the services would not be (or
-- would not have been) provided to the plaintiff but for the injury; and (d) the services are provided (or are to be
-- provided) for at least 6 hours per week; and (e) the services are provided (or are to be provided) over a period of at
-- least 6 months." (1): "must not ... award ... except in accordance with this section": JUST FALSE when one fails.
@export Can I get damages for the unpaid care my family or friends give me because of my injury (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 s 87W(1), (2))? Only if the court is satisfied that you reasonably need the care, the need arose solely because of the injury, it would not be given but for the injury, and it is (or will be) given for at least 6 hours a week over at least 6 months. Applies only where Part VIB applies in full.
GIVEN `my claim relates to` IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law` @desc Which Part of the Australian Consumer Law your claim relates to. One of: Part 2-1, Part 2-2, Part 2-3, Part 3-1, Part 3-2, Part 3-3, Part 3-4, Part 3-5, Part 5-4 Division 2, another provision
      `it results from tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death or injury results from smoking or other use of tobacco products
      `I reasonably need the care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied there is (or was) a reasonable need for the care (domestic, nursing, or care that eases the effects of the injury, which you do not pay for)
      `the need arose solely from the injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied the need arose solely because of the injury
      `it would not be given but for the injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied the care would not be given but for the injury
      `hours per week` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours a week the care is (or will be) given
      `months of care` IS A NUMBER @desc Over how many months the care is (or will be) given
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I get damages for the unpaid care I receive question` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco` `I reasonably need the care` `the need arose solely from the injury` `it would not be given but for the injury` `hours per week` `months of care` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`Part VIB applies in full` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco`))
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `I reasonably need the care` AND `the need arose solely from the injury` AND `it would not be given but for the injury` AND `hours per week` AT LEAST 6 AND `months of care` AT LEAST 6
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

-- ss 87W(3), (4) and 87X(3), (4): for services of at least 40 hours a week, no more per week than average weekly
-- earnings; for less than 40 hours, no more per hour than 1/40 of average weekly earnings. Given here as the most per
-- week: average weekly earnings at 40 hours or more, otherwise the hours times 1/40 of it.
GIVEN `hours per week` IS A NUMBER, `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`ss 87W, 87X -- the most per week for care` `hours per week` `average weekly earnings` MEANS
    IF `hours per week` AT LEAST 40
    THEN `average weekly earnings`
    ELSE `hours per week` TIMES (`average weekly earnings` DIVIDED BY 40)

@export What is the most per week I can be awarded for unpaid care (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 s 87W(3), (4))? For care of 40 hours a week or more, no more than average weekly earnings a week; for less, no more than 1/40 of average weekly earnings an hour. Average weekly earnings are for the quarter the care was given, or the latest published quarter for future care or where that quarter's figure was not yet published. Applies only where Part VIB applies in full.
GIVEN `my claim relates to` IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law` @desc Which Part of the Australian Consumer Law your claim relates to. One of: Part 2-1, Part 2-2, Part 2-3, Part 3-1, Part 3-2, Part 3-3, Part 3-4, Part 3-5, Part 5-4 Division 2, another provision
      `it results from tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death or injury results from smoking or other use of tobacco products
      `hours per week` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours a week the care was (or will be) given
      `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER @desc Average weekly earnings for the applicable quarter, in dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the most per week for the care I receive question` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco` `hours per week` `average weekly earnings` MEANS
    IF `Part VIB applies in full` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco`
    THEN JUST (`ss 87W, 87X -- the most per week for care` `hours per week` `average weekly earnings`)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 87X(2): "The court must be satisfied that: (a) prior to his or her loss of capacity to provide the services, the
-- plaintiff had provided the services: (i) for at least 6 hours per week; and (ii) over a period of at least 6 months;
-- and (b) the other person would have been entitled, if the plaintiff had died as a result of the contravention ..., to
-- recover damages under a law of a State or Territory for loss of the plaintiff's services." The caps of (3), (4) follow.
DECLARE `Care I gave answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: damages may be awarded, at most this much per week`  HAS `the most per week` IS A NUMBER
    `no: s 87X(2) is not met, so no damages for the care I can no longer give`
    `Part VIB's limits on damages do not apply to this claim`

@export I can no longer care for someone because of my injury: can I get damages for that, and up to how much (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 s 87X)? Only if, before the injury, you gave the unpaid care for at least 6 hours a week over at least 6 months, and the person you cared for could have recovered damages under State or Territory law for the loss of your services had you died. The most per week is average weekly earnings for 40 or more hours, otherwise 1/40 of it per hour. Applies only where Part VIB applies in full.
GIVEN `my claim relates to` IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law` @desc Which Part of the Australian Consumer Law your claim relates to. One of: Part 2-1, Part 2-2, Part 2-3, Part 3-1, Part 3-2, Part 3-3, Part 3-4, Part 3-5, Part 5-4 Division 2, another provision
      `it results from tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death or injury results from smoking or other use of tobacco products
      `hours per week I gave` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours a week you gave (or would have given) the unpaid care before the injury
      `months I gave it` IS A NUMBER @desc Over how many months you had given the care before the injury
      `they could have claimed had I died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you cared for would have been entitled under State or Territory law to damages for the loss of your services if you had died of the contravention
      `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER @desc Average weekly earnings for the applicable quarter, in dollars
GIVETH A `Care I gave answer`
`Can I get damages for the care I can no longer give question` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco` `hours per week I gave` `months I gave it` `they could have claimed had I died` `average weekly earnings` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`Part VIB applies in full` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco`))
    THEN `Part VIB's limits on damages do not apply to this claim`
    ELSE IF `hours per week I gave` AT LEAST 6 AND `months I gave it` AT LEAST 6 AND `they could have claimed had I died`
         THEN `yes: damages may be awarded, at most this much per week` (`ss 87W, 87X -- the most per week for care` `hours per week I gave` `average weekly earnings`)
         ELSE `no: s 87X(2) is not met, so no damages for the care I can no longer give`

-- s 87Y(1): the present value of a lump sum for future economic loss is found by applying "(a) a discount rate of the
-- percentage prescribed by the regulations; or (b) if no percentage is prescribed--a discount rate of 5%." The deposited
-- Competition and Consumer Regulations 2010 (Compilation No. 100) prescribe none: 5%.
@export What discount rate applies to the lump sum for my future economic loss (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 s 87Y)? 5% a year, since the regulations prescribe no other rate. Applies only where Part VIB applies in full and the award includes a lump sum for future economic loss.
GIVEN `my claim relates to` IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law` @desc Which Part of the Australian Consumer Law your claim relates to. One of: Part 2-1, Part 2-2, Part 2-3, Part 3-1, Part 3-2, Part 3-3, Part 3-4, Part 3-5, Part 5-4 Division 2, another provision
      `it results from tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death or injury results from smoking or other use of tobacco products
      `a lump sum for future economic loss` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the award is to include a lump sum for future economic loss of any kind
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What discount rate applies to my future loss question` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco` `a lump sum for future economic loss` MEANS
    IF `Part VIB applies in full` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco` AND `a lump sum for future economic loss`
    THEN JUST 5
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 87Z: the court must not award for loss of employer superannuation contributions more than "Superannuation percentage
-- x Damages for earnings loss" (formula from the sidecar); "superannuation percentage means the charge percentage (within
-- the meaning of subsection 17A(2) of the Superannuation Guarantee (Administration) Act 1992) divided by 100" -- borrowed,
-- an input.
@export What is the most I can be awarded for lost employer superannuation contributions (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 s 87Z)? No more than the superannuation guarantee charge percentage (as a fraction) times the damages for lost earnings or earning capacity on which the contributions are based. Applies only where Part VIB applies in full.
GIVEN `my claim relates to` IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law` @desc Which Part of the Australian Consumer Law your claim relates to. One of: Part 2-1, Part 2-2, Part 2-3, Part 3-1, Part 3-2, Part 3-3, Part 3-4, Part 3-5, Part 5-4 Division 2, another provision
      `it results from tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death or injury results from smoking or other use of tobacco products
      `damages for earnings loss` IS A NUMBER @desc The personal injury damages for past and future lost earnings or earning capacity on which the superannuation contributions are (or would be) based, in dollars
      `the charge percentage` IS A NUMBER @desc The superannuation guarantee charge percentage under s 17A(2) of the Superannuation Guarantee (Administration) Act 1992, as a number (for example 12 for 12%)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the most for my lost superannuation question` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco` `damages for earnings loss` `the charge percentage` MEANS
    IF `Part VIB applies in full` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco`
    THEN JUST ((`the charge percentage` DIVIDED BY 100) TIMES `damages for earnings loss`)
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Part VIB: interest, exemplary damages and structured settlements (Divisions 6-7)`

DECLARE `Kind of damages` IS ONE OF
    `non-economic loss`
    `gratuitous attendant care services for me`
    `loss of my capacity to provide gratuitous attendant care to others`
    `another kind (such as past lost earnings or medical expenses)`

-- s 87ZA(1): a court "must not ... order the payment of interest on personal injury damages for: (a) non-economic loss;
-- or (b) gratuitous attendant care services for the plaintiff; or (c) loss of the plaintiff's capacity to provide
-- gratuitous attendant care services to other persons." (2): "If ... a court is satisfied that interest is payable on
-- personal injury damages of another kind, the rate ... is: (a) the rate ... prescribed by the regulations; or (b) if no
-- rate is prescribed--the 10-year benchmark bond rate on the day on which the court determines the personal injury
-- damages." Whether interest is payable on another kind is the court's (and other law's): NOTHING.
@export Can I get interest on this kind of personal injury damages (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 s 87ZA(1), (2))? No interest on damages for non-economic loss, for unpaid care given to you, or for your lost capacity to care for others. For other kinds, if the court finds interest payable, the rate is the 10-year Commonwealth benchmark bond rate (no rate is prescribed by regulation). Applies only where Part VIB applies in full.
GIVEN `my claim relates to` IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law` @desc Which Part of the Australian Consumer Law your claim relates to. One of: Part 2-1, Part 2-2, Part 2-3, Part 3-1, Part 3-2, Part 3-3, Part 3-4, Part 3-5, Part 5-4 Division 2, another provision
      `it results from tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death or injury results from smoking or other use of tobacco products
      `the kind of damages` IS A `Kind of damages` @desc Which kind of damages. One of: non-economic loss; gratuitous attendant care services for me; loss of my capacity to provide gratuitous attendant care to others; another kind (such as past lost earnings or medical expenses)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I get interest on these damages question` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco` `the kind of damages` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`Part VIB applies in full` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco`))
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE CONSIDER `the kind of damages`
         WHEN `another kind (such as past lost earnings or medical expenses)` THEN NOTHING
         OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- s 87ZA(4): the 10-year benchmark bond rate on a day is "(a) if the day occurs on or after 1 March in a particular year
-- and before 1 September in that year--the ... rate ... applying on the first business day of January in that year; or
-- (b) otherwise--the ... rate ... applying on the first business day of July in the preceding year." Read literally (fork
-- F9): a day from 1 September to 31 December takes July of the year before, not of that year. The answer is the first day
-- of the month whose first business day sets the rate (business days need a holiday calendar, not deposited).
GIVEN `determined on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 87ZA(4) -- the month whose first business day sets the bond rate` `determined on` MEANS
    IF DATE_MONTH `determined on` AT LEAST 3 AND DATE_MONTH `determined on` AT MOST 8
    THEN `the calendar date` 1 1 (DATE_YEAR `determined on`)
    ELSE `the calendar date` 1 7 (DATE_YEAR `determined on` MINUS 1)

@export Which month's 10-year bond rate sets the interest on my personal injury damages (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 s 87ZA(2), (4))? If the court determines the damages between 1 March and 31 August, the rate applying on the first business day of January that year; otherwise, the rate applying on the first business day of July in the preceding year (read literally, even for a day in September to December). The answer is the first day of that month.
GIVEN `the court determines the damages on` IS A DATE @desc The day the court determines the personal injury damages
GIVETH A DATE
`Which month's bond rate sets my interest question` `the court determines the damages on` MEANS
    `s 87ZA(4) -- the month whose first business day sets the bond rate` `the court determines the damages on`

-- s 87ZB(1): "A court must not, in a proceeding to which this Part applies, award exemplary damages or aggravated damages
-- in respect of death or personal injury." Flat negative: JUST FALSE. (2): other loss, or other proceedings: unaffected
-- (NOTHING).
@export Can I get exemplary or aggravated damages for my injury (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 s 87ZB)? No, where Part VIB applies in full to the claim and the damages would be in respect of death or personal injury. Other loss, and other proceedings, are not affected.
GIVEN `my claim relates to` IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law` @desc Which Part of the Australian Consumer Law your claim relates to. One of: Part 2-1, Part 2-2, Part 2-3, Part 3-1, Part 3-2, Part 3-3, Part 3-4, Part 3-5, Part 5-4 Division 2, another provision
      `it results from tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death or injury results from smoking or other use of tobacco products
      `in respect of death or personal injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the exemplary or aggravated damages would be in respect of death or personal injury
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I get exemplary or aggravated damages question` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco` `in respect of death or personal injury` MEANS
    IF `Part VIB applies in full` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco` AND `in respect of death or personal injury`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 87ZC(1): "In a proceeding to which this Part applies, a court may, on the application of the parties, make an order
-- under section 87 approving a structured settlement ... even though the payment of damages is not in the form of a lump
-- sum". By s 87E(2) Division 7 reaches tobacco claims too, and by ss 137C(2), 137E(3) tobacco claims for Part 2-1 or 3-1.
-- JUST TRUE where Division 7 applies and the parties apply; NOTHING otherwise (a power, silent about other cases).
@export Can the court approve a structured settlement (periodic payments instead of a lump sum) for my injury claim (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 s 87ZC)? Yes, on the application of the parties, where Part VIB's structured-settlement Division reaches the claim (including tobacco claims).
GIVEN `my claim relates to` IS A `Part of the Australian Consumer Law` @desc Which Part of the Australian Consumer Law your claim relates to. One of: Part 2-1, Part 2-2, Part 2-3, Part 3-1, Part 3-2, Part 3-3, Part 3-4, Part 3-5, Part 5-4 Division 2, another provision
      `it results from tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death or injury results from smoking or other use of tobacco products
      `the parties apply together` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the parties (you and the defendant) apply for the court to approve the structured settlement
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the court approve a structured settlement question` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco` `the parties apply together` MEANS
    IF `Part VIB's time limits apply` `my claim relates to` `it results from tobacco` AND `the parties apply together`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
